+++ b/beintaskbot-main/docs/employee-ai-integration-scope-audit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,80 @@
+# AI and integration scope audit
+
+This is a code audit and an implementation boundary for the employee-rights specification, version 2. It is not a report of production configuration or a release approval. No real rights, credentials, connections, customer messages, or imports were changed during this work.
+
+## Implemented without expanding authority
+
+1. AI agents now have a separate, hidden `access_responsible_id`. Historical rows use the same one-time unambiguous company-owner migration as native documents. `created_by` remains historical, and new user-created agents store the authenticated creator as the responsible member. Browser patches cannot change that field.
+2. AI schema readiness checks include the ownership column, tenant-composite FK, index, and durable migration receipt. A cold schema repair does not reinterpret new NULL-owned system rows as historical documents.
+3. The AI configuration-options GET checks current database management authority before its optional Kommo catalog request. A logged-in session without AI-management authority does not trigger a provider request. Remote failure can still use the already authorized local catalog; a successful refresh reauthorizes in its store transaction.
+4. Tenant-wide handoff diagnostics, Connect diagnostics, and the unified AI-state snapshot are blocked for a member with an explicit narrow/denied dialog read scope until those aggregate projections are partitioned by source. Their existing legacy and tenant-wide paths are unchanged. This closes an indirect metadata bypass; it does not grant AI management through dialog rights.
+5. Shared integrations now publish independent CRUD scopes using the approved company-owner responsibility model. See [shared integration scopes](shared-integration-scopes.md) for current behavior and retained provider restrictions. The global employee-scopes rollout gate remains off by default.
+
+## Shared integration management
+
+The earlier undefined-ownership blocker is resolved: the current active company
+owner is responsible for each company-wide connection. Current-database
+self/department/all scopes apply separately to management reads, creation,
+updates, and deletion. Safe connection state remains visible to active members;
+credentials and arbitrary metadata are never part of that projection.
+
+Disconnect and key replacement require a company-impact confirmation. Provider
+constraints and stronger owner-only operations remain in force, including OAuth,
+Linear employee/publication/runtime rules, and protected Chat AI model settings.
+This is local implementation, not rollout permission or evidence of deployment.
+See `shared-integration-scopes.md` and its isolated tests for exact coverage.
+
+## AI agents: real operations and their indirect paths
+
+The core API has real create/list/update/delete operations. Updating `enabled` is an existing agent update, not a new independent grant. However, row-filtering only `list_agents` and `save_agent` would leave the following connected paths unprotected:
+
+- Agent triggers, teams, primary/expert bindings, and replacement/archive operations reference more than one agent.
+- Knowledge sources can be company-wide or agent-bound. Upload, archive, restore, deduplication, and publication have existing separate constraints.
+- Dashboard, reliability, handoffs, channel catalog, state snapshots, and deletion previews aggregate agents, dialogs, jobs, identities, or usage beyond a single agent row.
+- Virtual tests, simulations, historical replay, instructor/learning workflows, analysis campaigns, and AI-administrator drafts/approvals can read configuration and execute operations indirectly.
+- Pause/resume/handoff and dialog assignments require source access and independent AI authority. Broad AI-management privilege must not override a newly explicit narrow dialog scope. The dialog-scope implementation handles exact-source conversation endpoints separately from the aggregate guards above.
+- Company emergency stop, automatic sending, native CRM actions, extraction and provider spending are shared runtime controls. Permission over an employee-owned agent does not automatically authorize changing them for the company.
+- Permanent deletion currently requires company-owner authority, exact-agent confirmation, expected version, and safety checks against active work. It must not become an ordinary employee operation merely because a generic D cell exists.
+- Background workers execute under admitted jobs/runtime safety policy, not a current interactive employee request. Revocation, queued work, creator identity, and post-revocation execution must be resolved explicitly before using employee scopes as a worker policy.
+
+The common AI store `_authorize` currently means company owner or active admin with the existing `ai_agents_manage` flag. Many independent modules use it. Replacing it with one Boolean “some scoped action is allowed” would make view/create/update interchangeable and would bypass both resource scope and operation-specific authority.
+
+Primary sources: `tenant_ai_agents_store.py`, `tenant_ai_agents_api.py`, `tenant_ai_dashboard.py`, `tenant_ai_state.py`, `tenant_ai_reliability.py`, `tenant_ai_testing_store.py`, `tenant_ai_simulation_engine.py`, `tenant_ai_historical_replay.py`, `tenant_ai_instructor_store.py`, `tenant_ai_learning.py`, `tenant_ai_administrator_apply.py`, `tenant_knowledge_candidates_store.py`, `tenant_operator_assignment.py`, and `tenant_web_routes.py`.
+
+## Required decisions and remaining implementation
+
+- Shared integration ownership and company-impact behavior are implemented separately; keep source/dialog data rights independent from connection management.
+- Specify the treatment of shared AI/knowledge/runtime resources and cross-agent operations, including the retained owner-only safety actions.
+- Decide which related-dialog/AI aggregate information should remain available inside an authorized AI document versus its general section. Narrow-dialog users currently receive a deliberate blocked response for unconverted tenant-wide diagnostics.
+- Enumerate every interactive and background route for each registered action, apply the same effective scope, and compare rights before/after on a synthetic copy before activating the matrix.
+
+The current work supplies safe ownership metadata and closes concrete authorization gaps. Shared integration CRUD is implemented separately; AI CRUD is not complete.
+
+## Dormant AI document implementation (2026-11-11)
+
+This is an intermediate safety implementation, **not completed AI CRUD coverage or release approval**. `ai_agents` remains absent from `SCOPE_ACTION_CATALOG`, so the normal employee editor and validated settings writer cannot publish it. The global gate remains off by default. The internal `tenant_ai_scoped_access` evaluator accepts synthetic database AI matrices for tests/future integration only. It intentionally does not change the shared catalog.
+
+Implemented in the core store:
+
+- Independent create, view, and update scopes evaluate current database membership, policy, responsible employee and department in the same transaction. Policy/member/agent/department read locks fence those decisions. Cached browser role and settings do not authorize access. Missing action overrides retain existing legacy semantics; explicit denial never falls back.
+- New agents belong to the authenticated employee; create scope is evaluated against that employee. Wizard retry and legacy wizard recovery check the existing row's current responsible employee before returning or modifying it, not just historical `created_by`.
+- List filters agent documents and hides responsibility metadata. Scoped lists omit unpartitioned expert bindings and assigned-dialog totals. Trigger reads require parent-agent view access and omit referenced helper IDs.
+- Enable/disable is an update. Shared runtime consent, credentials and company flags are not granted by any agent scope. Explicit narrow dialog read blocks scoped configuration writes until source-aware runtime configuration is implemented.
+- Cross-agent bindings, expert diagnostic tools and shared dispatch configurations are deliberately blocked for scoped writers, including preservation of existing such configuration during a patch. This is a temporary safety block, not a product decision to remove those features.
+- Generic `_authorize` entrypoints do not infer shared management from any AI CRUD grant, even all four `all` grants. Explicit AI matrices close the unconverted settings/options, knowledge, dashboard, diagnostics, test/learning/instructor and similar common-gate workflows. Existing owner authority and gate-off behavior remain unchanged.
+
+### Not implemented / must remain gated
+
+1. Employee delete has no defined safe operation. Current archive requires a replacement and transfers dialogs, modifies other teams/triggers, and cancels work. Both archive and permanent deletion remain owner-only. Smallest product decision: should employee D permit archive only for an unused/unbound agent, with no transfers or cross-agent rewrites? No such operation was invented here.
+2. Cross-agent team/binding writes, trigger writes, shared and agent-bound knowledge mutations, provider configuration options, diagnostic projections, virtual tests, simulations, historical replay, learning/instructor and administrator workflows still need individually scoped contracts. Their generic management gates fail closed for synthetic scoped actors. This is not evidence that every separate legacy permission entrypoint has been converted.
+3. No scoped actor is newly admitted into those test/learning workflows. Already-admitted background execution is **not fully converted**: testing rechecks `_authorize` at completion, but simulation/runtime worker paths use admitted execution policy and do not uniformly carry/recheck the requesting employee's matrix. Product semantics must distinguish revoking interactive management from withdrawing an already approved company runtime or paid job. No automatic cancellation of existing company runtime was added.
+4. Real PostgreSQL concurrent revocation/ownership races must run in isolated CI. PGlite validates SQL and transaction-path behavior but cannot establish multi-connection race coverage.
+5. No UI eligibility was enabled. Publication must wait for complete route/worker inventory, defined D semantics, scoped shared-workflow contracts, integration tests, and synthetic before/after comparison.
+
+Local regression coverage: `tests/test_ai_scoped_documents.py` uses disposable PGlite and blocked provider/DB fallbacks. It covers scopes and NULL owners, foreign tenants, independent actions, forged cached role, membership/rights/department revocation, ownership-bound create/retry, enable/disable, helper/diagnostic safety blocks, and owner/gate-off behavior.
+
+### Follow-up transaction ordering
+
+Scoped updates acquire the existing tenant configuration advisory mutex before taking the agent row `FOR UPDATE`; they do not take `FOR SHARE` and later upgrade. This also preserves ordering against wizard recovery, which takes the mutex before touching agent rows. Existing setup-key retries are read-only (`FOR SHARE`); legacy recovery selects candidate rows `FOR UPDATE` before changing a setup key, so its ownership recheck does not introduce a shared-lock upgrade.
+
+`tests/test_ai_scoped_documents_postgres.py` adds real multi-connection tests for competing versioned updates, mutex-before-row ordering against recovery, revocation committed before admission, and an admitted update fencing revocation until commit. They require a disposable localhost `BEINTASKBOT_TEST_POSTGRES_DSN` and use actual `pg_blocking_pids` observations rather than timing alone. Local result: 24 PGlite tests passed; these 4 PostgreSQL tests skipped because no DSN was available. CI must run them before relying on race coverage.
