@@ -0,0 +1,38 @@
+# Native CRM scoped access
+
+Implementation is behind `TENANT_SCOPED_ACCESS_ENABLED`; it is not an approval to enable the rollout. With the gate off, the contact directory uses its original company/deal routes and guards; the new narrow contact relation routes reject with `scoped_relation_unavailable` (403). Company-card contact unlink keeps its legacy delete check when off. The server explicitly supplies the UI relation mode and row-specific company relation capabilities; the browser never infers rollout from role or a guessed scope. The native sections are `deals`, `customers` (contacts), and `companies`. An absent cell retains its previous native CRUD permission, without converting a provider permission into an all-record scope.
+
+## Covered boundaries
+
+- Native contact collection, direct contact read, creation, update, and archival.
+- Source-qualified contact directory: native authority is applied before search, source totals, pagination, and row action flags. Kommo identities, pipeline restrictions, account provenance, and read-only actions remain separate.
+- Native deal collection/detail, creation, update, archival, and form options. Options are usable for a permitted create/update action independently of view. Contact options respect the activated contact scope.
+- Native company collection/detail, creation, update, archival, and related lists/counts. Activated contact/deal scopes restrict their embedded lists and counts without leaking inaccessible IDs via totals.
+- Every HTTP handler forwards the authenticated actor to its store. The store rereads current active tenant/membership, workflow grants and department under database fences. Writes authorize and lock the target inside the same transaction. Department mutations use the common tenant fence; department-scoped writes also lock and recheck the responsible membership.
+- New human-created records receive immutable `access_responsible_id=actor_id`. Existing `responsible_id` retains its previous operational assignment meaning. Client payloads never set access ownership. Idempotent creates under the matrix cannot return another creator's document.
+
+A readable deal includes its existing contact and company information even when those directories are closed. That embedded context does not grant independent client updates. Updating an existing deal's contact link (including unlinking) requires only the update scope of that deal. Updating a contact card's deal link uses the contact-specific `link_deal` / `unlink_deal` action and requires only that contact's update scope, not deal update or either delete permission. This narrow route changes no other deal field and returns no deal contents. Unlink checks that the deal currently points to the exact authorized contact. The target always stays inside the same tenant; unrelated deal lists are not expanded by this permission.
+
+The company selector and company tab inside an existing contact card use the contact-specific `link_company` / `unlink_company` actions. Both require only that contact's update scope, even when company view/update/delete and contact delete are denied. The transaction locks the authorized contact and a tenant-bound active company, changes only `contact.company_id` and its modification time, and returns a narrow relation receipt with no company fields. Unlink is conditional on the exact current company. The company row, including its modification time and hidden owner, is never changed. A denied company collection does not expose alternative targets; the UI can still detach the already known company. Creation alone does not grant permission to change a company link: the separate post-create link operation also requires contact update authority.
+
+With the gate on, company-card contact link/unlink similarly requires only U of the source company, never contact R/U/D or company D. Both directions lock the contact before the company, preventing an opposite-direction lock cycle. The target contact stays tenant-bound and active; unlink checks its exact current company. Only its company relation and modification time change. The company card does not expand the contact list or contact fields; a denied target list does not break the readable company card. Company→deal operations remain outside this approved rule.
+
+## Explicit rollout blockers
+
+1. Company→deal relationship semantics remain unapproved. Their legacy links require customers:update plus deals:update, and unlinks require customers:delete. When either section is scoped, they still return `scoped_relation_not_configured` (409). Initial contact selection during scoped deal creation and changing deal channel/conversation links also remain explicit blockers. Existing deal↔contact, contact→company and company→contact card updates follow the approved source-card U-only contract only with the gate enabled.
+2. Trusted integration/AI/background stores do not have an authenticated human actor. They retain their separate existing integration authority and create NULL access owners, visible only to an all scope. Deciding how future automatically created documents acquire a human responsible person remains necessary before a complete rollout. Historical-owner backfill is owned by the shared schema migration, not by this code.
+3. The provider queue exports a full configured record and has no confirmed field-specific relation/unlink event, idempotency or actor-revocation contract. New narrow relation routes never enqueue a full-record sync. If the modified entity type currently has outbound or bidirectional synchronization with nonempty allowed fields, these operations return `scoped_relation_sync_not_configured` (409) before any mutation; the UI explicitly says the link was not changed. This is a temporary safe blocker, not a release-ready synchronization design. Provider-specific relation-only API semantics remain under independent investigation; no real provider calls were made.
+   Confirmed provider boundaries from read-only documentation research:
+   - Kommo has relation-only [link](https://developers.kommo.com/reference/linking-entities), [unlink](https://developers.kommo.com/reference/unlinking-entities) and [filtered relation read-back](https://developers.kommo.com/reference/linked-entities) endpoints. A contact-card deal change must operate through the lead endpoint, not an unsupported contact→lead endpoint. [Cardinality](https://developers.kommo.com/docs/subject-area) differs from the local single contact FK: a lead can have multiple contacts. Whether to replace the primary contact or add/remove a relation therefore cannot be inferred from the local FK alone. Documents do not promise idempotent repeated POST, atomic company replacement or conflict-free retries after a timeout.
+   - The current company entity is absent from `ENTITY_TYPES` and the external identity table's entity-type constraint. Existing mappings lack provider-account/integration-generation binding. Company relations cannot be sent safely by guessing an external ID or reusing an identity after reconnection.
+   - BEIN ERP's current implementation PATCHes `crm/leads/{id}` with `partner_id`; a confirmed relation-only clear/unlink payload and provider idempotency guarantee have not been established from [its documentation](https://docs.beinsystems.az/docs/api/). Existing client headers alone are not proof of server behavior.
+   - A durable safe relation event would additionally need exact actor, source-card U action, old/new targets, account-bound identity and expected relation state, with permission revalidation before delivery and explicit unknown/conflict recovery. The current full-record outbox contains no such contract. These are implementation prerequisites plus unresolved provider semantics, not permission to send other fields.
+   - An [inactive relation contract](crm-relation-sync-contract.md) now provides strict, immutable event serialization, account/generation-bound identity checks and pure read-back classification. It is not imported by runtime code, creates no outbox or mapping schema and does not remove this blocker. Provider interpretation, transactional persistence, current authorization checks and the delivery/recovery chain still need implementation and verification.
+
+4. CRM-specific frontend behavior for independent record actions and scoped-relationship 409 responses still needs the wider rollout review. The existing contact-directory browser regressions are distinct from full scoped-matrix UI acceptance.
+
+## Verification
+
+`tests/test_crm_scoped_access_postgres.py` uses disposable localhost PostgreSQL and rejects provider traffic through the shared isolated fixture. It covers independent CRUD for all four scopes in three sections, two tenants/departments, list/search/count/pagination authorization, linked client context, creator ownership, unchanged operational assignment, stale HTTP profiles, revoked membership, suspended tenant, concurrent permission revocation, idempotent replay, fail-closed unapproved relationships, contact-card company linking with company R/U/D denied, exact conditional unlink, foreign/archived targets, narrow HTTP payloads and receipts, current U revocation, unchanged company data, company-card contact U-only operations, both gate-off bypasses, row-specific relation capabilities, permission/department revocation, opposite-direction lock ordering, and active outbound blocks with unchanged synthetic outbox state.
+
+The existing native CRUD, source-qualified directory, AI native action, WhatsApp identity and browser directory suites remain regression targets. Never use a production DSN or send test messages to real contacts.
